        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1022",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" ON target=\"_blank\" LINK. At line 41 json2html builds an outbound anchor from JSON-supplied data: `html += '<a href=\"' + json + '\" class=\"json-string\" target=\"_blank\">' + json + '</a>'`. The link opens a new browsing context chosen by the (untrusted) JSON author but carries no `rel` attribute at all -- the link-hardening control is entirely absent from the scan. Consequences: (a) the full URL of the embedding page is leaked to the attacker-chosen third-party origin via the Referer header (no `noreferrer`), and (b) on browsers that do not apply the modern implicit-noopener default, the opened document receives a live `window.opener` handle and can navigate the viewer page (reverse tabnabbing). Missing control = `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` on the generated target=_blank anchor.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived from source. json-viewer/jquery.json-viewer.js:41 emits `html += '<a href=\"' + json + '\" class=\"json-string\" target=\"_blank\">' + json + '</a>'` \u2014 a new-browsing-context link whose href comes from caller-supplied (potentially untrusted) JSON, with no `rel` attribute at all. The control is absent repo-wide: grep for noopener|noreferrer|rel= over the whole tree yields only `rel=\"stylesheet\"` in demo.html:8 and README.md:30, and nothing in any .js. The branch is enabled by default (`withLinks: true`, line 115). The library's own unit test at test/jquery.json-viewer.test.js:16 asserts the exact rendered output `<a href=\"http://www.hello.com\" class=\"json-string\" target=\"_blank\">` \u2014 proving the missing rel is present in real emitted HTML, not merely in a string literal. Reachability holds in source via the public API $.fn.jsonViewer (109) -> json2html (122) -> $(this).html(html) (128); the graph's reachable_from_entry:false on lines 122/128 is the documented anonymous-callback (`this.each(function(){...})`) under-linking, so I do not downgrade on it. Severity is LOW, not higher: I corrected two overstatements in the lead \u2014 the default Referrer-Policy (strict-origin-when-cross-origin) leaks only the origin cross-origin, not the full URL, and all current browsers apply implicit noopener to target=_blank, so reverse tabnabbing requires a legacy engine. Genuine defense-in-depth gap (CWE-1022). Exploit-reference grounding unavailable: version pinned exactly at 1.4.0 (package.json:3) but exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit applies-or-patched signal either way."
          },
          "locations": [
