        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In json2html(), the HTML-escaping protection is applied ONLY to the string-value branch and is absent on the object-key branch, so attacker-controlled JSON object keys reach a jQuery .html() sink unescaped (DOM XSS). At line 32-33 there is an explicitly-labelled protection ('// Escape tags and quotes') that entity-encodes &, <, >, ' and \" for string VALUES. The sibling object branch at lines 80-86 builds `var keyRepr = options.withQuotes ? '<span class=\"json-string\">\"' + key + '\"</span>' : key;` and then `html += '<a href class=\"json-toggle\">' + keyRepr + '</a>'` (line 84) / `html += keyRepr` (line 86) with the raw `key` -- no escape is applied anywhere on that path. The resulting `html` flows into `$(this).html(html)` at line 128. Because `withQuotes` defaults to false (line 114), the default path is line 86, which injects the raw key directly. The protection exists in the same function but was never factored into a shared helper (no htmlEscape/sanitize method exists in the file), so the key path bypasses it entirely. Re-derive: render `$('#x').jsonViewer({'<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>': 1})`.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source independently. json2html() applies HTML entity-encoding ONLY in the `typeof json === 'string'` value branch (lines 33-38, under the explicit comment `// Escape tags and quotes`, plus a second value-only pass at line 44). The sibling object-key branch builds `keyRepr` from the raw `key` at lines 80-81 and appends it to `html` at line 84 (`withQuotes:true`) or line 86 (default) with no escaping on either arm. I read the whole 158-line file: the only `.replace()` calls in it are lines 34-38 and 44, all inside the value branch, and no htmlEscape/sanitize helper exists, so the key path bypasses the protection entirely. `html` reaches the jQuery sink `$(this).html(html)` at line 128 (graph confirms FLOWS_TO {arg_index:1} from the json2html call at line 122 to the line-128 sink; all key-path calls have reachable_from_entry=true). The default `withQuotes: false` (line 114) selects line 86, the fully-raw arm. The repo's own test suite independently proves the raw emission: test/jquery.json-viewer.test.js:30 asserts default output is literally `<li>hello: ...`, so a key of `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` is injected verbatim into .html(). CHANGELOG.md:4,9,24 shows three prior XSS hardenings, all on the value path and none on the key path, confirming the asymmetric/incomplete-protection shape. Advisory exploit-reference check could not be performed: version pinned exactly at jquery.json-viewer 1.4.0 (package.json:3) but exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no module/CVE/version comparison was possible; the verdict rests solely on source and graph evidence I gathered."
          },
